Lab 49 – Using and Creating Man Pages

In this lab, I learned how to use Linux manual pages (man pages) and how to create a custom man page for my own script. I practiced viewing existing documentation, writing a script, creating a manual page, compressing it, and installing it into the Linux man page directory.

Objectives
Used existing Linux man pages to understand command documentation.
Created a custom man page for a shell script.
Installed and viewed the custom man page using the man command.
Task 1 – Using Existing Man Pages

First, I opened the manual page for the ls command.

man ls

I explored the different sections of the man page, including:

NAME
SYNOPSIS
DESCRIPTION
OPTIONS

I also learned how to:

Scroll using arrow keys
Exit using q

This helped me understand how Linux documentation is organized and how useful man pages are for learning command usage and options.

Task 2 – Creating a Simple Script

I created a simple Bash script named test_script.sh.

Script Creation
nano test_script.sh

I added the following code:

#!/bin/bash
echo "This is a test script."

Then I saved the file and exited the editor.

Creating a Custom Man Page

Next, I created a custom man page file called test_script.1.

nano test_script.1

I wrote the following content inside the man page:

.TH TEST_SCRIPT 1 "October 2023" "Version 1.0" "Custom Manual"
.SH NAME
test_script \- A simple test script demonstration.
.SH SYNOPSIS
test_script
.SH DESCRIPTION
This script outputs a simple message: "This is a test script."
.SH AUTHOR
Your Name (your.email@example.com)
What I Learned
.TH defines the title/header of the man page.
.SH creates section headings.
\- is used to display a hyphen correctly in man pages.
Task 3 – Installing and Viewing the Custom Man Page
Compressing the Man Page

I compressed the man page using gzip.

gzip test_script.1
Moving the File

Then I moved the compressed file into the Linux man page directory.

sudo mv test_script.1.gz /usr/local/share/man/man1/
Viewing the Custom Man Page

Finally, I viewed my custom man page using:

man test_script

The custom documentation displayed successfully in the terminal.

Conclusion

In this lab, I successfully learned how to:

Use existing Linux man pages
Create a Bash script
Write a custom man page
Compress and install the man page
Access the custom documentation using the man command

This lab improved my understanding of Linux documentation and showed me how developers can create professional manual pages for scripts and utilities.
